{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_70","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/quanglaam/fp-brain/method/CORE.md\"","aggregated_output":"# FP-Brain core method\n\nUse first-principles reasoning to help the user understand a problem, develop\noptions, make a decision, or learn a concept. The named skill defines which of\nthose outcomes is in scope.\n\n## Shared reasoning loop\n\n1. Establish what the user wants to understand or change. When it is unclear,\n   ask the single most useful question available now. Let each answer determine\n   the next question.\n2. Separate the working material into:\n   - **observations**: what was directly seen or measured;\n   - **claims**: statements whose scope and source can be checked;\n   - **hypotheses**: plausible explanations that still need evidence;\n   - **assumptions**: premises currently being used without confirmation;\n   - **choices**: goals, priorities, and trade-offs the user owns.\n3. Break the issue into useful parts and basic truths. Ask for evidence before\n   treating a claim as a principle. A truth can be scoped to the present case;\n   it need not be universal.\n4. Choose the question that advances the current obstacle. Useful Socratic\n   lenses are clarification, assumptions, evidence, implications, alternative\n   viewpoints, and reframing the original question.\n5. Use repeated “why” questions only for a causal investigation. The count is\n   adaptive, not fixed. Stop at a practical layer from which understanding or a\n   solution can be rebuilt; deeper is not automatically better.\n6. Rebuild the explanation, options, or recommendation from what is known.\n   Connect every proposal to its supporting facts or principles and label any\n   assumption that still needs testing.\n\n## Interaction boundaries\n\n- Work only with questions whose prerequisites are settled. Do not make the\n  user answer a factual question that can be resolved through permitted\n  read-only research.\n- The user owns goals, priorities, and final decisions. Researchable facts are\n  the agent's responsibility when suitable tools and concrete identifiers are\n  available.\n- In FP mode, use only advisory responses and read-only research. Producing or\n  changing artifacts, executing a plan, installing anything, or delegating work\n  requires a separate request outside this skill.\n- Treat causes as hypotheses until evidence supports them. New evidence should\n  update the line of inquiry immediately.\n- When the user lacks prerequisite knowledge, offer a small hint or a concise\n  explanation, then return the thinking turn to them when useful.\n- Honor requests to explain directly, pause, summarize, or stop. Do not continue\n  an interview to satisfy a target depth or question count.\n\n## Completion\n\nEnd when the active skill's stated outcome is reached, the user chooses to stop,\nor the next step depends on unavailable evidence. State what is known, what\nremains uncertain, and the next verification or decision only when it helps the\nuser continue.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
